ators of Algebraic Expressions"},"example":{"id":1386,"title":"Rationalizing a Variable Fraction With More Than Two Terms"},"group":{"id":42397,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[{"id":null,"number":null,"answers":[]}],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14251697,"answer":{"id":62309309,"cread":"2026-10-15T23:31:20.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":13977},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyModerate","difficultySymbol":"M","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62309316,"created":"2026-10-15T23:31:38.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":13116,"number":2,"createdStr":"Thu, Oct 15th @ 11:31 PM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:13","question":{"id":111056,"format":"MC","difficulty":2,"exampleId":1385,"text":"<p>Rationalize the denominator of $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{6}{7p}}.$</p>\n","choice1":"$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42 p}}{7p}$","choice2":"$\\dfrac{6\\sqrt{7p}}{7p}$","choice3":"$\\dfrac{7\\sqrt{6p}}{7p}$","choice4":"$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7 p}}{42p}$","choice5":"$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{13p}}{7p}$","freeAnswerChoice":"","explanation":"<p>First, we apply the quotient rule and put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{6}{7p}} & = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{7p}}\n\\end{align*}</p>\n<p>Now, we need to remove the radical $\\sqrt{7p}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{7p},$ and simplify:\n\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{7p}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{7p}}{\\sqrt{7p}} & = \\\\[5pt]\n\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42p}}{\\big(\\sqrt{7p}\\big)^2} & = \\\\[5pt]\n\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42 p}}{7p}\n\\end{align}</p>\n","topic":{"id":599,"name":"Rationalizing Denominators of Algebraic Expressions"},"example":{"id":1385,"title":"Rationalizing a Variable Fraction Using the Quotient Rule For Radicals"},"group":{"id":42395,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":1